  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Another low pressure system is expected to \nimpact the area Monday into Tuesday, bringing strong winds, \nbrief coastal flooding concerns, and rain. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nOn Sunday, a deep upper level trough will be centered over the \nOhio Valley and will swing an accompanying low pressure towards \nMaine, strengthening as it tracks across the northeast. The \nexact track of the system remains uncertain at this time, but \nmodel guidance continues to suggest the low will pass somewhere \nover Maine on Monday. Strong winds will be the primary concern \nwith this system, but the low location will determine how strong\nthe winds will be. The latest forecast has the surface low \npassing just north of Bangor as it moves to the northeast. If \nthe low is further north, winds will be weaker, and if the low \nis more off shore, winds will also be weaker and less onshore. \nWith the low currently expected to be just inland, the southeast\nwinds Monday afternoon could produce some brief coastal \nflooding concerns with the afternoon high tides. As the low \nquickly moves to the northeast, winds will become west-\nnorthwesterly and blowing offshore. Precipitation will mainly be\nin the form of rain, with some snow mixing in across the north.\nThe timing and track of the low will continue to become clearer\nas we get closer to the event.\n\nBehind the low center, winds turn west-northwesterly funneling \ncold air into the region. As the low exits the region, it \ncontinues to strengthen, meanwhile a high pressure builds in \nbehind it. This will increase the pressure gradient, increasing \nwinds. Cold advection and colder air aloft will allow strong \nwinds aloft to mix down to the surface through Tuesday morning \nleading to elevated gusts across the area. There is an elevated \nthreat of power outages. Again, the timing and track of the \nupper level low will determine the strength of these wind gusts.",
